// std/cstring.jtr — an owned, NUL-terminated copy of a `str`, for the C APIs
// that insist on one.
//
// ─── Why this exists ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Jestyr's `str` is a length-carrying view: `{ptr, len}`, no terminator. C's
// string APIs are the other convention, and `.cstr` bridges the gap for a string
// LITERAL only — the literal's bytes are in static storage with a NUL already
// after them, which is exactly why it works and exactly why it does not
// generalise. So until this module, `std/file.open` could only open a path
// written into the source, which is not much of a filesystem API.
//
// The runtime has had the answer all along: `jestyr_rt_cpath` mallocs `len + 1`,
// copies, and writes the NUL — it is how the `read_file` intrinsic accepts a
// computed path. This module is that helper, in the language, with an owner.
//
// ─── Ownership is the whole design ────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// A `cstr` is a bare pointer with no length and no owner. Handing one out is
// therefore handing out a borrow, and the borrow has to be anchored to something
// — which is what this type is. `as_cstr` returns a view valid exactly as long as
// the `CString` is, and the `Drop` impl is what makes "as long as" mean something
// a reader can check.
//
// It is deliberately NOT a `String` replacement: no push, no concat, no growth.
// A `CString` is a one-shot conversion at the FFI boundary, and giving it string
// operations would invite it inland, where `str`/`String` are the right types and
// the terminator is dead weight.
//
// ─── The interior-NUL hazard, reported rather than hidden ─────────────────────
//
// A Jestyr `str` may contain a zero byte; a C string may not. The copy here is
// faithful, so a `str` with an interior NUL produces a `CString` whose C view
// STOPS EARLY — silently, because that is what C does. Rather than pretend
// otherwise, `has_interior_nul` lets a caller ask, and every function that hands
// a path to the OS should. `std/file.open_path` does.
//
// Truncating instead would be worse: `open_path("a\0../../etc/passwd")` opening
// `a` is a security answer, not a correctness one, and the caller who built that
// string is the only one who can say what it meant.
//
// Run the tests:  jestyrc test examples/std/cstring_test.jtr

import "mem"

// An owned NUL-terminated byte buffer. `n` is the length WITHOUT the terminator,
// so it matches the source `str`'s `len` and never has to be adjusted by callers.
pub struct CString {
    p: *mut u8,
    n: usize,
    a: Allocator,
}

// Copy `s` into a fresh NUL-terminated buffer.
//
// Always succeeds: an empty `str` gives a one-byte buffer holding just the
// terminator, which is a valid empty C string rather than a null pointer. That
// matters because C APIs distinguish "" from NULL, and returning the latter for
// an empty path would turn a bad argument into a crash.
@no_os pub fn from_str(read a: Allocator, read s: str) -> CString {
    var p: *mut u8 = mem.alloc_bytes(a, s.len + 1)
    var i: usize = 0
    for i < s.len {
        unsafe { (p + i).* = s[i] }
        i = i + 1
    }
    unsafe { (p + s.len).* = 0 }
    return CString{ p: p, n: s.len, a: a }
}

// The C view. Valid exactly as long as this `CString` is — do not store it past
// the owner's lifetime, which is the one rule a `cstr` cannot enforce for itself.
@no_os pub fn as_cstr(read c: CString) -> cstr {
    return c.p as cstr
}

// The length WITHOUT the terminator — the same number the source `str` reported.
@no_os pub fn len(read c: CString) -> usize {
    return c.n
}

@no_os pub fn is_empty(read c: CString) -> bool {
    return c.n == 0
}

// Does `s` contain a zero byte before its end? Such a `str` cannot round-trip
// through C: the terminator is in the middle, so every C consumer sees a shorter
// string than Jestyr does. Ask this BEFORE handing a computed string to the OS.
//
// This takes a `str` rather than a `CString` on purpose — the useful moment to
// ask is before the copy, when the caller still has somewhere to put the error.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn has_interior_nul(read s: str) -> bool {
    var i: usize = 0
    for i < s.len {
        if s[i] == 0 { return true }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return false
}

// Explicit teardown. Idempotent, so it composes with the automatic drop — the
// `std/list` contract.
@no_os pub fn free(mut c: CString) {
    if c.p != null {
        mem.free_bytes(c.a, c.p)
        c.p = null
        c.n = 0
    }
}

// Both teardown paths call `mem.free_bytes` DIRECTLY, and that is worth a note
// because until recently they could not.
//
// A module-qualified call inside a trait-`impl` method body used to emit `j_mem`
// as though the module binding were a local, and gcc failed with "'j_mem'
// undeclared" — resolution, typeck and the escape checker all passing first. The
// cause was that impl method bodies were never inferred at all, so nothing ever
// recorded the qualified resolution. This module carried a `drop_cbuf` indirection
// as a documented workaround, with a deliberately unshared name because a
// COLLISION-canonicalized name broke in the same body for the same reason.
//
// The workaround is gone because the bug is (see the Tier 2 note, §1.6). Its
// removal is the point: `impl Drop` is where the language frees raw memory, so a
// `Drop` body that can finally call across a module boundary — and that is now
// escape-checked, which it also never was — is the useful proof that the fix holds
// on real library code rather than on an eight-line reproducer.
impl Drop for CString {
    fn drop(mut self) {
        if self.p != null { mem.free_bytes(self.a, self.p) }
    }
}
